WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.575
Time dilation is one of the inherent qualities in relativity.

00:00:04.575 --> 00:00:10.495
That where you have gravitation there's a warp in time

00:00:10.495 --> 00:00:15.155
that your time relative to a frame of reference

00:00:15.155 --> 00:00:19.295
(to another place where you have less of

00:00:19.295 --> 00:00:23.615
a gravitational attraction or less mass)

00:00:23.615 --> 00:00:26.395
that time is going to be moving more slowly.

00:00:26.395 --> 00:00:32.435
Well that seems like the basis for 
some sort of time management.

00:00:32.435 --> 00:00:36.615
That you could use gravity to manage time

00:00:36.615 --> 00:00:38.555
by way of time dilation where

00:00:38.555 --> 00:00:41.255
where you had more gravity and

00:00:41.255 --> 00:00:43.183
where others had less gravity

00:00:43.183 --> 00:00:45.935
your time would be moving more slowly

00:00:45.935 --> 00:00:46.855
relative to them.

00:00:46.855 --> 00:00:50.955
And so I decided that I would try to market this.

00:00:50.955 --> 00:00:54.495
And I did so by creating time ingots.

00:00:54.495 --> 00:00:57.305
It seemed logical enough. I founded a company

00:00:57.305 --> 00:00:59.221
called Spacetime Industries.

00:00:59.221 --> 00:01:02.095
And these "time ingots" were as you can see...

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:05.579
well, they were bars of lead that i bought on eBay.

00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:07.695
And I packaged them though, and...

00:01:07.695 --> 00:01:10.115
The thing about this technology is that

00:01:10.115 --> 00:01:11.775
you could very conveniently

00:01:11.775 --> 00:01:13.835
put one of these on your desk.

00:01:13.835 --> 00:01:17.295
And you would have as your frame of reference

00:01:17.295 --> 00:01:19.735
ever-so-slight an advantage

00:01:19.735 --> 00:01:22.635
over the person in the next cubicle. So...

00:01:22.635 --> 00:01:27.775
Granted we're talking about a very very small advantage.

00:01:27.775 --> 00:01:29.595
But then I realized there were many other ways

00:01:29.595 --> 00:01:32.455
in which to use this sort of advantage

00:01:32.455 --> 00:01:37.455
that came courtesy of gravity and of Mr. Einstein.

00:01:37.455 --> 00:01:40.995
Namely you could have what I came to call

00:01:40.995 --> 00:01:42.995
The Time Warp Undershirt.

00:01:42.995 --> 00:01:47.615
A Time Warp Undershirt puts greater density,

00:01:47.615 --> 00:01:51.095
more mass, around your heart relative

00:01:51.095 --> 00:01:53.755
to the rest of your body. Especially your brain.

00:01:53.755 --> 00:01:56.215
And as a result you end up with this

00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:59.455
very slight advantage of living just that little bit longer

00:01:59.455 --> 00:02:03.015
relative to how long you think you're living.

00:02:03.015 --> 00:02:04.615
Or you get to think a little bit faster

00:02:04.615 --> 00:02:07.395
relative to how long you're living.

00:02:07.395 --> 00:02:11.255
I will grant you that this is a minimal effect.

00:02:11.262 --> 00:02:14.935
We're talking about perhaps a billionth

00:02:14.935 --> 00:02:17.115
of a billionth of maybe a billionth of a second

00:02:17.115 --> 00:02:18.735
over the course of the entire length of

00:02:18.735 --> 00:02:20.635
time that the universe has been here.

00:02:20.635 --> 00:02:25.775
So you really need in order to be able to feel this

00:02:25.775 --> 00:02:28.915
and to take advantage of this in a serious way

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:31.875
you need... well you need a neutron star.

00:02:31.875 --> 00:02:34.855
So that's what I came up with

00:02:34.855 --> 00:02:38.235
as a way to move forward in this technology.

00:02:38.235 --> 00:02:39.755
Namely I came up with a sort of an

00:02:39.755 --> 00:02:42.875
architecture that could be built on neutron stars.

00:02:42.875 --> 00:02:45.215
What I called an Elevator House.

00:02:45.215 --> 00:02:47.355
And the concept of the Elevator House is basically that

00:02:47.355 --> 00:02:48.755
every room was on a shaft.

00:02:48.755 --> 00:02:50.535
And you could arrange your life by the way

00:02:50.547 --> 00:02:51.715
that you arranged the rooms

00:02:51.715 --> 00:02:53.235
on these different shafts.

00:02:53.235 --> 00:02:55.675
So as to be able to fully manage your time.

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:58.515
And of course up on the roof is the garden

00:02:58.517 --> 00:03:00.975
where plants are growing relatively quickly

00:03:00.975 --> 00:03:02.395
compared to your life.

00:03:02.405 --> 00:03:06.235
And you're able therefore to have strawberries,

00:03:06.235 --> 00:03:09.106
as many as you want whenever you want them.

00:03:09.115 --> 00:03:11.875
But this is all still sort of selfish. And

00:03:11.875 --> 00:03:16.815
still sort of not fully managing time

00:03:16.815 --> 00:03:18.855
to the full potential i don't believe.

00:03:18.855 --> 00:03:20.575
I think that where it gets interesting

00:03:20.581 --> 00:03:22.615
is where it gets communal.

00:03:22.615 --> 00:03:26.115
That is to say not to manage time just for yourself,

00:03:26.115 --> 00:03:29.815
but what if an entire community had time management

00:03:29.815 --> 00:03:32.135
in this relativistic way?

00:03:32.135 --> 00:03:34.035
Well, I decided to design some cities

00:03:34.035 --> 00:03:35.835
I decided to go into urban planning. Why not?

00:03:35.835 --> 00:03:37.735
I'm an experimental philosopher after all.

00:03:37.735 --> 00:03:40.335
And so what I did was I designed a few cities.

00:03:40.335 --> 00:03:43.835
Now for my cities what i did was i used

00:03:43.835 --> 00:03:48.115
the equivalence of velocity to mass

00:03:48.115 --> 00:03:49.747
because it was easier at least it was

00:03:49.755 --> 00:03:51.095
easier to make some blueprints.

00:03:51.095 --> 00:03:53.375
And so i ended up with cities like this.

00:03:53.375 --> 00:03:56.415
The cities have different districts that are spinning

00:03:56.425 --> 00:04:00.278
very very fast but at different rates
relative to each other

00:04:00.278 --> 00:04:03.275
those of you who know clockmaking

00:04:03.275 --> 00:04:04.755
are able probably to figure out that

00:04:04.755 --> 00:04:06.195
what's going on right here

00:04:06.195 --> 00:04:08.475
is that the residential district is

00:04:08.475 --> 00:04:10.835
spinning at a rate that is going to give you

00:04:10.835 --> 00:04:12.855
a relatively long life span

00:04:12.855 --> 00:04:15.715
compared to... those are trees. That's

00:04:15.715 --> 00:04:17.425
the agricultural district on the other side.

00:04:17.425 --> 00:04:18.495
So you can eat well.

00:04:18.495 --> 00:04:21.255
And in the middle is the industrial district which

00:04:21.255 --> 00:04:25.075
frankly we do need our iPhones

00:04:25.075 --> 00:04:27.695
and other plastic things. But not too many of them.

00:04:27.695 --> 00:04:28.775
So it seemed like it really belongs

00:04:28.775 --> 00:04:30.355
in the middle. And so I then went ahead and

00:04:30.355 --> 00:04:32.635
then I came up with some other cities

00:04:32.635 --> 00:04:34.055
that could also be built

00:04:34.055 --> 00:04:37.195
along these lines. And submitted these out into the

00:04:37.195 --> 00:04:38.675
world. Totally open source.

00:04:38.675 --> 00:04:42.435
For any government that wished to take it on

00:04:42.435 --> 00:04:44.075
and to build one of these.

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:45.795
Nobody has done it yet

00:04:45.795 --> 00:04:49.495
I have to tell you. But it is also interesting to me

00:04:49.495 --> 00:04:53.315
that it got quite a lot of attention.

00:04:53.315 --> 00:04:55.235
That for instance Architizer

00:04:55.235 --> 00:04:58.175
one of the architectural trade publications

00:04:58.175 --> 00:04:59.655
wrote it up. And not only them but

00:04:59.655 --> 00:05:01.215
also The Wall Street Journal.

00:05:01.215 --> 00:05:03.895
And this is interesting to me.

00:05:03.895 --> 00:05:06.855
I mean not only is it a excuse for me to boast.

00:05:06.855 --> 00:05:08.795
It also is interesting from the standpoint

00:05:08.795 --> 00:05:10.495
of what i was talking about earlier.

00:05:10.495 --> 00:05:14.955
Namely how to do philosophy in public in a way that

00:05:14.955 --> 00:05:18.115
engages people in a broad conversation.

00:05:18.115 --> 00:05:21.415
Where what it's about kind of

00:05:21.415 --> 00:05:24.095
comes out of the conversation itself.

00:05:24.095 --> 00:05:26.955
And so that you end up with, in the case

00:05:26.955 --> 00:05:29.475
of a project like this, all sorts of

00:05:29.475 --> 00:05:30.895
things falling out of it.

00:05:30.895 --> 00:05:32.515
All sorts of ideas that I certainly

00:05:32.515 --> 00:05:34.055
didn't have going into it. And

00:05:34.055 --> 00:05:36.315
I probably ended up stealing from other people

00:05:36.315 --> 00:05:38.335
as I was having conversations with them.

00:05:38.335 --> 00:05:41.295
But really in terms of how we think about time

00:05:41.295 --> 00:05:45.333
in our society, and the way in which

00:05:45.335 --> 00:05:47.275
the arrangement of time

00:05:47.275 --> 00:05:50.775
and the understanding of time in communal terms

00:05:50.775 --> 00:05:54.135
fundamentally changes time as experienced

00:05:54.135 --> 00:05:56.515
and how society can be built

00:05:56.515 --> 00:05:59.135
in ways perhaps not using quite

00:05:59.135 --> 00:06:01.975
the same amount of elaborate clockwork as I've done.

00:06:01.975 --> 00:06:04.255
But in ways that might be truer

00:06:04.275 --> 00:06:08.935
to the relationship that we have through time.

